WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.240
Hello I’m kind of Andrew Heaton

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.100
and you’re watching Mostly Weekly.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:04.900
(Mostly.)

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.580
And today I’d like to talk to you about
minimum wage.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.960
Every year I explain why I think minimum wage
is a bad idea,

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.300
that way I can use hate mail to heat my house.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.060
Now the minimum wage is back in the news thanks
to places like Seattle,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.880
where the city has increased their minimum wage
from $11 an hour to $13.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.560
And in New York, where a law was just passed
raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:34.080
Listen, I’m on team helping poor people!
#PovertySucks

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.500
But we shouldn’t make policies based on
what their intention is,

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.019
we should judge them on what their effect
is.

00:00:40.019 --> 00:00:42.579
The effect of jacking up the federal minimum
wage

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:45.300
is that employees cost an employer more money.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.539
So they’re apt to cut back on hours, reduce
jobs,

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:50.630
or replace employees with robots.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.750
And increasing the minimum wage does lead
to more automation,

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.200
at least according to evil right-wing think
tank MIT.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.260
To give you an example, this is what happened
when I asked for a raise:

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.420
I’d like a raise.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.500
We’re replacing you with a robot.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.580
Alright here he is.

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:05.990
Mandroid Heaton.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:07.520
Greetings evil human.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:08.640
Hello Mandroid.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.880
Can you tell us a little bit about the effects
of minimum wage on labor markets?

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.060
It’s great.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.340
Are you just saying that because you think
a higher minimum wage will lead to more robots?

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:19.440
No, of course not.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.860
I’m saying that people can’t live off
less than $15 an hour

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.740
and it’s inhumane to vote otherwise.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.080
Hold on, I’m not saying poverty isn’t
bad,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.200
I’m saying that if raising the minimum wage
means you don't get a job at all,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.840
then the real minimum wage is zero.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:34.680
You’re heartless.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.100
And I think you humans you should jack it
up to $30.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.500
Hell, why not $60!

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.980
Okay, if we raised the minimum wage it would
help some people

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.480
but would also lay off a lot of other people.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.380
According to the Congressional Budget Office,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.760
raising the minimum wage to just $10.10 an
hour

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.680
would lift about a million people above the
poverty threshold, great.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.800
But it would also mean laying off half a million
workers.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.820
In other words, increasing the minimum wage helps people who have low skills,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.280
but it screws over people with the lowest amount
of skills.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.380
Okay, you need to think big picture.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.140
Step1: Raise the minimum wage.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:11.160
Step 2: Build Skynet.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.700
Is there a Step 3?

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:13.880
You’ll find out.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:15.760
Okay.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.140
Picture a ladder.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:21.180
Raising the minimum wage is like kicking the
bottom rung off that ladder,

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.900
and then bragging about making the bottom
rung higher.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:29.260
That’s true—but people that are below
that new step don’t get jobs at all.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.740
Plus minimum wage jobs aren’t permanent
castes poor people are stuck in.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.599
They’re mostly entry-level jobs.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.760
Two thirds of minimum wage workers move on
to a higher salary within a year.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.520
About half of minimum wage workers are sixteen
to twenty-five years old.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.440
The more we raise the minimum wage, the more
of them we lock out of the job market.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.180
Which is really bad news for a high school
dropout

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.100
or a felon looking to get their foot in the
door.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.840
And maybe some big companies can afford a
higher minimum wage.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.640
But mom and pop shops can’t.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.319
For example, Starbucks can afford to pay their
employees more:

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.840
they’re a giant company and they could just
charge more to spell your name correctly.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.620
Local coffee shops don’t have that option--

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.940
they have razor thin profit margins.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.880
Labor is a significant portion of their overhead.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.420
So if you raise the minimum wage,

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.360
there’s a real chance you’ll drive independent
businesses out of business—

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.659
which helps out big companies,

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:17.400
many of which want a higher minimum wage for
exactly this reason.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.940
In Seattle businesses have increased the hourly
wage of their workers,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.860
but they've also cut the amount of hours they
work.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.020
So workers on net are making less money.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:32.280
And again, I can’t stress this enough, the
higher we raise the minimum wage,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.620
the more robots we have to worry about.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.060
It’s a bad idea to have one one-size-fits-all
price for the entire country.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.380
You can pay two dollars for a beer in Scabies
County, Virginia.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.580
Whereas in New York City if you want to buy
a Pale Ale

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.160
you need to kidnap and ransom a toddler.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.159
Stuff costs more in big cities.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.320
So a grocery store in Gout City, Montana

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.640
just can’t afford to pay $15 an hour to all of its cashiers.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.260
Listen, we all have human feelings.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.080
And we all consume carbon.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.940
But--Sarah?

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.200
The teleprompter is all robot-y.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.180
You been replaced by a robot?

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:05.180
No.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:06.819
Okay.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:11.120
Well anyway, Remember: spay and neuter your
Roomba.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.160
I’m Andrew Heaton and I’m definitely a
human being and not a robot.

